{
  "id": "c16",
  "title": "malloc and free",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c16-l1",
      "title": "Asking for Memory",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "So far, every array had a fixed size, written into the program. But often you only learn how much memory you need while the program runs.",
        "malloc (from stdlib.h) asks for memory while running: int *a = malloc(n * sizeof(int)); gives room for n ints. It returns a void *, an address without a type, which C turns into an int * for you.",
        "After that, a works just like an array: a[0], a[1], … The memory stays yours until you give it back."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <stdlib.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int n = 5, i;",
          "    int *a = malloc(n * sizeof(int));",
          "    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)",
          "        a[i] = i * i;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < n; i++)",
          "        printf(\"%d \", a[i]);",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    free(a);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "0 1 4 9 16 "
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
