    {
      "id": "c16-l2",
      "title": "Giving It Back",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Memory from malloc stays taken until you call free(p). A program that keeps asking and never frees has a memory leak, and can slowly eat all the memory of the machine.",
        "After free(p), the memory isn't yours anymore. Using it again (use after free) or freeing it twice (double free) are classic, dangerous bugs.",
        "Only free what malloc gave you, and free it exactly once. A common pattern: a function that returns new memory, and a caller that frees it when done."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <stdlib.h>",
          "",
          "#include <string.h>",
          "",
          "char *copy_of(char *s) {",
          "    char *c = malloc(strlen(s) + 1);",
          "    strcpy(c, s);",
          "    return c;",
          "}",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char *name = copy_of(\"thompson\");",
          "    name[0] = 'T';",
          "    printf(\"%s\\n\", name);",
          "    free(name);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "Thompson"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c16-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is a memory leak?",
          "options": [
            "Memory that's never given back with free",
            "A crash",
            "Reading past an array",
            "A slow loop"
          ],
          "answer": 0,
          "explanation": "The memory stays taken until the program ends."
        },
        {
          "id": "c16-l2-e2",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "Why does copy_of ask for strlen(s) + 1 bytes?",
