    {
      "id": "c16-l3",
      "title": "When malloc Fails",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Memory isn't endless, especially on a 1970s PDP-11 with a few hundred kilobytes. When malloc can't find enough, it returns NULL. Careful programs check: if (a == NULL) { … }.",
        "calloc(n, size) is malloc's sibling: it asks for n elements of the given size, and fills them with zeros. malloc's memory instead starts with leftover garbage.",
        "exit(1) from stdlib.h ends the program at once, with 1 meaning \"something went wrong\"."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "#include <stdlib.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int i;",
          "    int *count = calloc(5, sizeof(int));",
          "    if (count == NULL) {",
          "        printf(\"out of memory\\n\");",
          "        exit(1);",
          "    }",
          "    count[2]++;",
          "    count[2]++;",
          "    count[4]++;",
          "    for (i = 0; i < 5; i++)",
          "        printf(\"%d \", count[i]);",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    free(count);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "0 0 2 0 1 "
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c16-l3-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What does malloc return when there's no memory left?",
